this is madagascar's capital  antananarivo  much of it submerged underwater after  days of unrelenting rainfall  two storms sent water rushing through  these streets  resident pierre michelle shows the  damage left in the wake  the power is cut in this area we have to  deal with that and the floods we're  never at peace everyone is affected not  only my home but all the houses in this  neighborhood  neighbor vivian does her best to clear  the water  her family is staying for now  billiard fans are neat the water came in  and we didn't know how to get it out of  the house so we put the kids on the bunk  bed my husband and i then sorted through  our stuff to see the damage  local officials are asking people to  leave their homes  and join thousands of others at  established shelters  we've set up the first shelters to  receive the victims and the municipality  will take care of them we call on all  disaster victims to leave their homes  and go to the shelters  this school gymnasium is providing  short-term refuge  most of those who escaped say their  homes were swept away or destroyed  part of our house has collapsed we ask  for help we can't live at home because  it's flooded if we leave here we have  nowhere to go  our house was flooded twice the first  time we stayed there but this time we  had to leave because the mattresses are  wet and our baby got sick  it's not unusual to see heavy rain  during the wet season but this was  exceptional  with more than 100 millimeters of  rainfall in just one day  20 districts are now on high alert  and people living in impoverished areas  with poor drainage are more vulnerable  to flooding  many rescue teams have now taken boats  to navigate the high waters  others are organizing fresh water  supplies hygiene packs and food  as many are now vulnerable to waterborne  diseases  from the sky it's clear how much work  needs to be done  as thousands of homes remain submerged  and the forecast shows more rainfall now  on the horizon 